---

<Article
  title="Symmetry in programmable matter"
  kicker="Science · Distributed algorithms"
  status="Interactive"
  headline="Finding symmetry when every particle is blind"
  deck="Tiny robots on a hexagonal grid, each with a few bits of memory and only its neighbours in view, work out together whether their shape is symmetric. Play with every step below."
  facts={[
    { label: 'Where', value: 'Paderborn University, project group “Programmable Matter”' },
    { label: 'When', value: 'Aug 2024 – Aug 2025' },
    { label: 'Team', value: 'Ahmet Can Kaytaz, Ibrahim Ali Khan, Pooja Garg, Rasika Jayavelu, Thevin Arokiaraj Lawrence' },
    { label: 'Model', value: 'Geometric amoebot model with reconfigurable circuits' },
    { label: 'Simulator', value: 'AmoebotSim (Unity, C#)' },
    { label: 'Cost', value: 'Prime generation O(log⁵ m) rounds; identity test O(log⁴ m), for a chain of m amoebots' },
  ]}
  cites={['amoebots']}
>
  <p>
    Imagine a swarm of robots so small that each one can store only a handful of bits. They sit on a
    hexagonal grid, see only their immediate neighbours, and talk by beeping. Can they work out, together,
    whether the shape they form is symmetric? Our project group built the answer as a working system in
    the AmoebotSim simulator.<Cite id="amoebots" />
  </p>

  <h2>The question</h2>
  <p>
    Start with the shape. Draw your own swarm below, then turn or flip it. A symmetric swarm lands exactly
    on its own ghost.
  </p>

  <SwarmFigure />

  <p>
    That was easy because the computer saw the whole picture. No amoebot ever does. Each has constant
    memory, a shared sense of direction, and a few “pins” it can wire to its neighbours. Wired together,
    those pins form circuits: a beep sent into a circuit reaches every amoebot on it in the same round.
  </p>

  <h2>From a shape to a string</h2>
  <p>
    The trick is to turn geometry into bits. Pick a direction d, elect the amoebot furthest along it as the
    root, and tour the swarm with a fixed rule: always try neighbours clockwise, starting from d. Write every
    step down. Do the same from a turned direction d′. If the swarm survives that turn, the two write-ups are
    identical.
  </p>

  <TourFigure />

  <p>
    Now the hard question has become a cleaner one: are two bit strings equal? The strings are laid out on a
    straight chain, one bit of each per amoebot.
  </p>

  <h2>Beeping a number down the chain</h2>
  <p>
    Rather than compare the strings directly, the algorithm compares short fingerprints of them, a classic
    randomised trick. For that, the chain splits into blocks of k = O(log m) amoebots. The first ℓ amoebots
    of every block, its parameter slice, hold a prime p and a random number r, one bit each. Getting r from one block into every other block is what my
    SendValueToBlocks subroutine does.
  </p>

  <BeepFigure />

  <p>
    Two configurations alternate. All passive: every amoebot connects its neighbours’ pins straight
    through, so the chain becomes two long circuits and one beep reaches all blocks. Singleton: every pin
    is cut loose, so each marker can nudge its neighbour and the reading position moves one step. The number
    of rounds depends on ℓ, not on how many blocks there are.
  </p>

  <h2>Fingerprints instead of comparisons</h2>
  <p>
    Read a bit string x as a polynomial: f<sub>x</sub>(r) = Σ x<sub>e</sub> · r<sup>e</sup> mod p. Equal
    strings always give equal fingerprints. Different strings agree only if r happens to be a root of
    their difference, which is rare, and repeating with fresh values of r makes it rarer still. Each block
    evaluates its own terms in parallel with modular exponentiation, then the blocks add their sums into the
    head of the chain. That computation, ComputeF, was the other part I built.
  </p>

  <FingerprintFigure />

  <h2>My part</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Random r:</strong> every amoebot in the parameter slice draws one bit; a distributed
      comparison rejects the candidate unless r &lt; p, and the loop repeats until it passes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>SendValueToBlocks:</strong> the two-round broadcast in Fig. 3, reusable for any value one
      block needs to share.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>ComputeF:</strong> per-block terms a<sub>e</sub>·r<sup>e</sup> mod p by right-to-left
      modular exponentiation, the cross-block sum f(r), and SubDivision2, which handles the case where the
      dividend is smaller than the divisor.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>With teammates:</strong> the canonical trees and symmetric shape generators behind Figs. 1
      and 2 (with Ahmet Can Kaytaz and Thevin Arokiaraj Lawrence), and a stand-in prime for testing (with
      Ahmet Can Kaytaz).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Pooja Garg built the block-leader election that divides the chain; Thevin Arokiaraj Lawrence and Rasika
    Jayavelu built distributed prime generation; Ahmet Can Kaytaz tied everything into the end-to-end run and
    led the write-up.
  </p>

  <h2>What I learned</h2>
  <p>
    Thinking distributedly was hard at first. Every amoebot is blind to the global state, so you design local
    rules and trust them to produce the global answer. Once that clicked, the constraint became the
    interesting part.
  </p>
</Article>
